,{"claim":"rows[519]","confidence":"inferred","entity_id":"person_facts","entity_type":"decision","locator":null,"source_id":"st_cyr_register_ichr_v4","use":"research"},{"claim":"rows[821]","confidence":"inferred","entity_id":"person_facts","entity_type":"decision","locator":null,"source_id":"st_cyr_register_ichr_v4","use":"research"},{"claim":"superseded_record.arrival","confidence":"inferred","entity_id":"hh_owen_thomas","entity_type":"decision","locator":null,"source_id":"st_cyr_register_ichr_v4","use":"research"},{"claim":"superseded_record.persons[owen_thomas]","confidence":"inferred","entity_id":"hh_owen_thomas","entity_type":"decision","locator":null,"source_id":"st_cyr_register_ichr_v4","use":"research"},{"claim":"superseded_record.present_on_scene_date","confidence":"inferred","entity_id":"hh_owen_thomas","entity_type":"decision","locator":null,"source_id":"st_cyr_register_ichr_v4","use":"research"}],"source":{"archived_url":"","citation":"The first Chicago Catholic church register — Father John Mary Irenaeus St. Cyr's marriage register and his death and burial page — as printed and annotated in the *Illinois Catholic Historical Review*, vol. 4 (July 1921 - April 1922), and republished in two pages by the Genealogy Trails Transcription Team. 128 dated marriages of 1834-1839, each naming groom, bride, witnesses and officiating priest, and 11 deaths and burials of June 1834 to March 1837.","source_id":"st_cyr_register_ichr_v4","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","transcribes":[{"date":"1833-1839","note":"THE ORIGINAL, and nobody in this project has seen it. What is read here is a 1921-22 article that prints the marriage entries and the death entries and comments on them. The article itself says the death record 'is contained on a single page' of the book.","tier":1,"work":"The first Chicago Catholic church record, kept by Father J. M. I. St. Cyr from 1833 and continued by Fathers Bernard Schaeffer and Timothy O'Meara to 1839"},{"date":"1921","note":"The layer actually read, at one further remove: the Genealogy Trails team transcribed these two pages out of the Review and this project read the transcription. Every record citing this source is graded transcription_mediated, twice over.","tier":2,"work":"Illinois Catholic Historical Review, vol. 4 (July 1921 - April 1922)"}],"url":"https://genealogytrails.com/ill/cook/marriages_catholic.html","what_it_does_not_supply":["any address, lot, dwelling or workplace — the register places nobody anywhere in the town","any age, except for three infants and one nine-year-old on the death page","the baptisms. The article tallies 282 of them by these same priests and prints none; that register is T-0503's object and is not in this source","residence. A marriage at Chicago on a day of 1835 is presence on that day, and presence is not residence","reliable spelling. The article says so itself: a French and German priest writing Irish and English names, and an Irish priest writing French and German ones"],"what_it_supplies":["128 marriages at Chicago (125 of them) between 1834 and 1839, each dated, and each naming its witnesses BY NAME — 513 named readings of people standing at a named place on a named day","six marriages IN THE SCENE YEAR, which with their witnesses put twenty named people in Chicago on five days of 1835","eleven deaths and burials, four of them in 1835, including the death of Thomas Owen, agent of the Indians, at Chicago on 15 October 1835","the register's own arithmetic — 22 St. Cyr marriages, 18 Schaeffer, 87 O'Meara, 1 Plunkett — which verifies any reading of it without the book","the dates bounding each priest's time at Chicago, which is what makes an unsigned entry attributable","the Irish, French and German Catholic population of the town, which is the part the poll books and letter lists reach least well"]},"uses":["scene","research"]}
